# Cloud — our control-plane dashboard, as a workbook

Our cloud dashboard is the **Studio** — a Svelte 5 SPA in `studio/`, built to `app.html` + `assets/`
and served by the nexus at `/cloud`. (The old v1 — a vanilla `client` island, `island.work` + `app.js`
+ `app.css` + `views/` — has been removed; the Studio replaced it entirely.) This `server` block is the
Studio's backend: the `/cloud/*` API it calls, plus the resources below. The shell is the rail + sidebar;
surfaces (Members, Usage, Storage, Secrets, Billing, MCP, …) render in the Studio from real platform data.

**Auth seam.** The island authenticates to the control plane (`/api/platform`) with our OWN native
session cookie (`Nexus.Auth.Session`, set by `/auth/login`). No session ⇒ the platform API answers
401 and the island bounces to `/login/`. The `work` CLI uses a `wbk_` PAT on the same `/api/platform`
— one auth path for browser and CLI; nothing about auth is baked into the runtime.

## The data — dogfooded straight off the platform

The dashboard reads the platform's own primitives via `/api/platform/*` (the hosted control plane,
`Nexus.Platform`). This `server` block additionally exposes `usage`/`fleet`/`storage` live sources
backed by `Nexus.Capacity` for the dogfood nexus serving it, and mints real CLI tokens.

resource Event do
  description "The activity feed — every bus #event the dashboard persists (who did what, where, when)."
  tags ["system", "activity"]
  kind :string
  actor :string
  title :string
  target :string
  tags :string
  scope :string
  at :integer
end

# A durable agent-run record — one row per agent run (tenant-scoped SQLite). Written when a run
# finishes (streamed live over the `agent_run` Live source); read back by the Studio Runs view.
resource Run do
  description "Agent run history — one row per agent run (who launched it, the brief, status, tokens)."
  tags ["system", "agents"]
  rid :string       # run id (stable handle, distinct from the storage row id)
  agent :string     # which named agent ran
  user :string      # who launched it
  task :string      # the brief
  status :string    # ok | error | timeout
  turns :integer    # LLM turns taken
  tokens :integer   # total tokens (from telemetry)
  answer :string    # final answer (truncated for the list view)
  started :integer  # unix seconds
  ended :integer    # unix seconds
  # The ownership ledger (epic wb-kodp): a runtime-counter-signed metering attestation (Nexus.Ledger
  # encode form) anchoring the tokens/model spend so analytics can be VERIFIED, not self-reported. When
  # the run produced a workspace commit, the same attestation is also a git note keyed by that commit.
  attest :string    # encoded signed metering attestation (Nexus.Ledger.encode), or "" if unsigned
  author :string    # the author's did:key, when the run was signed by a per-user key (slice 2); else ""
end

# The task/plan store — the orchestration backbone. A task is a unit of work; a plan is a parent
# task with child subtasks (parent = parent's tid). Dispatching a task launches a Run and links its
# rid. THE LINE: the bus + `task` effect are generic; this store + the Studio board are our cloud.
resource Todo do
  description "The task & issue board — units of work and filed problems the orchestration dispatches."
  tags ["system", "orchestration"]
  tid :string        # stable task id
  kind :string       # "task" (work to do) | "issue" (a problem an agent/human filed) — one fabric
  title :string      # short summary
  detail :string     # the brief / acceptance (for an issue: the failure context + proposed fix)
  status :string     # open | in_progress | blocked | done | cancelled
  agent :string      # assignee (a named agent), or "" if unassigned
  parent :string     # parent task tid for plan decomposition, or "" for a root task
  priority :integer  # 0 (top) .. 4 (backlog)
  created_by :string # who/what filed it (a user, or an agent name)
  run :string        # the rid of the dispatched run (a task) or the FAILING run (an issue)
  created :integer   # unix seconds
  updated :integer   # unix seconds
end

# Scheduled agent runs — the time arm of the orchestration fabric. A schedule fires a named agent on a
# brief via Nexus.Scheduler (the durable OTP scheduler, same fire path as hook triggers). Append-only,
# folded by sid (latest wins) like Todo; re-armed on boot. THE LINE: the scheduler is the open standard;
# persisting + managing these schedules is our cloud product.
resource Schedule do
  description "Scheduled agent runs — the time arm of orchestration (cron/every/at), re-armed on boot."
  tags ["system", "orchestration"]
  sid :string        # stable schedule id
  agent :string      # the named agent to run
  task :string       # the brief
  mode :string       # every | cron | at | after
  spec :string       # the value for the mode: "15m" | "0 9 * * 1" | "14:00"
  owner :string      # who created it
  created :integer   # unix seconds
  enabled :integer   # 1 armed, 0 cancelled
end

# The personal profile store — who a person IS on this nexus, decoupled from auth. One folded row per
# user (latest by `updated` wins, same fold pattern as Todo). Identity (name/email/role) still comes
# from the session /me; THIS holds the editable, expressive layer: avatar, tagline, bio, links, accent.
# Nexus-agnostic — the same person carries a fresh profile per nexus they join. THE LINE: the store is
# generic mechanism (any deployer's users get a profile); the "You" surface that renders it is our cloud.
resource Profile do
  description "Personal profiles — the editable identity layer (avatar, bio, links) per user, per nexus."
  tags ["system", "account"]
  uid :string      # stable user key (the signed-in email) — the fold key
  display :string  # chosen display name (overrides the session name when set)
  tagline :string  # one-line headline shown under the name
  bio :string      # a short paragraph about themselves
  avatar :string   # avatar image (a data: URL or an uploaded asset path); "" = initial glyph
  accent :string   # a DNA pastel var name for their initial glyph, e.g. "peach" | "blue" | "mint"
  location :string # free-text locale ("San Francisco", a timezone, …)
  links :string    # newline-delimited "label|url" pairs (NO json — blocks are the source of truth)
  updated :integer # unix microseconds — the fold picks newest
end

# Author device keys (epic wb-kodp, slice 2): one identity, many device keys. Each surface a person
# signs from — browser (WebCrypto), CLI/editor/Claude Code (bound to the wbk_ PAT), nexus agents — holds
# its own Ed25519 device key, registered here under the user via proof-of-possession (Nexus.Authorship).
# An edit signed by ANY non-revoked device key of a user is provably theirs. Append-only, folded by did
# (latest by `registered` wins, so a revoked row supersedes the active one).
resource DeviceKey do
  description "Author device keys — the per-surface signing keys registered under each user's identity."
  tags ["system", "account"]
  uid :string        # the owning user (signed-in email), lower-cased
  did :string        # the device's did:key (its public key, self-certifying) — the fold key
  label :string      # human label ("Shane's laptop", "Chrome on macOS")
  surface :string    # browser | cli | editor | agent
  registered :integer # unix seconds (fold picks newest)
  revoked :integer   # 0 active, 1 revoked
end

# The Inbox saved-views store — a user's filter presets in the Inbox funnel (the email-style filter
# next to search). One folded row per (uid, name); newest by `updated` wins (same fold as Todo). NO
# JSON: the filter spec is a compact "key=val;key=val" string of dimensions (kind/actor/status/
# workspace/tags), parsed on read. Per-user, tenant-scoped; the default presets ship in code.
resource SavedView do
  description "Inbox saved views — a user's filter presets (kind/actor/status/workspace/tags)."
  tags ["system", "activity"]
  uid :string      # owning user (signed-in email) — part of the fold key
  name :string     # the view's display name (the other part of the fold key)
  emoji :string    # a leading glyph for the funnel entry
  filters :string  # compact "kind=failed,needs_you;actor=workhorse;status=open" spec (NO json)
  position :integer # sort order in the funnel
  updated :integer # unix microseconds — the fold picks newest
end

# Per-user inbox read-state — the email layer over the shared activity feed. One folded row per
# (uid, subject); `subject` is the thread key (a run id / session id / task id) the Inbox groups by.
# Holds read/unread, archived, and a cleared-needs-you flag. Append-only, folded by {uid,subject}.
resource InboxState do
  description "Per-user inbox state — read / archived / cleared flags keyed by thread subject."
  tags ["system", "activity"]
  uid :string      # the user this state belongs to
  subject :string  # the thread key (run id / session id / task id)
  read :integer    # 1 = the user has opened this thread
  archived :integer # 1 = archived out of the default Inbox (a new event un-archives it)
  cleared :integer # 1 = the needs-you flag was explicitly resolved/cleared
  updated :integer # unix microseconds — the fold picks newest
end

# Persist every bus event to the log. The reactive primitives (hook kind, #event, effect engine) are
# the open standard; this hook + the `log` effect are our cloud opting into a durable feed.
hook :activity do
  match tags: [:event]
  log
end

# ── Workspace composition (the Studio sidebar) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An authored layer over auto-discovery: declared items get this order/grouping/nesting; everything
# else is still auto-discovered and appended. `contains` nests an existing unit under another (shown
# in its drawer with a link icon, not duplicated at top level); `hide` drops a unit from the list.
# Unknown to the workbook compiler (skipped) — read only by /cloud/items.

workspace do
  # A flat, fully CUSTOM order — not grouped by kind, not alphabetical. Reorder freely; add `group "…" do
  # … end` only where YOU want a labeled section, `hide "…"` to drop one, `contains` to nest one inside
  # another. `general` is the default channel (always provided; `hide "general"` to remove it).
  item "general"

  item "agents/workhorse.work"
  item "agents/autopilot.work"
  item "agents/research.work"
  item "agents/deck.work"
  item "agents/video.work"
  item "agents/graph.work"
  item "beachhead.work"

  item "agents/washy-production-plan.work" do
    contains "agents/washy-threat-model.work"
    contains "agents/washy-transpiler-research.work"
  end
  item "design/studio-v2.work"

  item "chat.work"
end

